From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stage C <stage-c@example.invalid>
Date: Wed, 19 Nov 2026 21:40:00 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: omit all-zero active data segments from the output

Linear memory starts zeroed, so an active data segment whose bytes are all
zero (for example a `.bss` section from a wasm object) does not need to be
encoded. wasm-ld leaves these implicit; Wild currently copies every input
segment into the output, which adds a fixed 16,388-byte all-zero segment to
every linked CogWasm module.

Omit an ordinary active segment when it has no data relocations and every
byte is zero. Keep its memory range reserved: `memory_cursor` still advances
by the full input length, so later segment offsets, data symbol addresses,
`__data_end` and the initial memory page count are unchanged. The layout
entry stays in `object_data_layouts` and keeps its `Active` segment place,
so code relocations that point into the omitted range still resolve. Counts,
offsets and `memory.init`/`data.drop` indices are renumbered only over emitted
segments.

Do not omit passive, TLS or shared-memory initialization segments: they are
referenced by segment index. Do not omit anything when the output imports its
memory via `--import-memory`, because the host-provided memory may already
contain data and Wild does not synthesize a `memory.fill` initializer for it
(this matches wasm-ld's `emitBssSegments` rule).

Tested with a wasm object containing a 16 KiB zero `.bss` and a nonzero
`.data` tail: output shrinks from 16,792 to 397 bytes, the zero segment is
gone, the tail keeps its offset and linear memory still has 2 pages. The
new `wasm/zero-data` integration test asserts both the segment count and
that no active segment is entirely zero, and runs the module under wasmtime.

This patch was changed by the contributors to CogWasm for the pinned Wild
checkout (0.11.0, fd79578abb). Wild is dual-licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0; this
patch is provided under the same terms.
